# Your agent loop

**Keep all student-created loop code and configuration in this folder.**

This is where you build the system that coordinates the coder, reviewer,
and tester. The implementation is yours to design; no loop is provided.

## What belongs here

- The orchestrator and its start/resume commands.
- Role definitions, prompts, and handoff schemas.
- MCP server/client code, tool definitions, and configuration.
- Role permissions, sandbox policies, and network allowlists.
- Dependency manifests and lockfiles for the loop.
- Loop-specific documentation and configuration examples without secrets.

Use subfolders when they help. For example:

```text
loop/
  README.md        # This file
  prompts/         # Instructions for each role
  loop.sh          # Bash loop
  agents.yml	   # Docker Agent team spec
```

These names are suggestions. Choose your own structure inside `loop/` and
document the entry point, dependencies, and configuration it needs.
Resolve paths relative to the repository so the loop works in the sandbox.

## Where everything else goes

| Content | Location |
| --- | --- |
| Saved state, handoffs, logs, findings, and run evidence | `artifacts/` |
| Port implementation and browser page | `platform/`, `web/` |
| Verification code, including checks for the loop itself | `tests/` |
| Game data | `assets/` |
| Generated build output | `build/` or `dist/` |
| Shared build/toolchain files | Repository root |

Keep reusable prompt templates here; save the actual tasks and responses
from each run in `artifacts/`. Keep secrets out of the repository and sandbox.
----
# Run artifacts

**Agents must use this folder for durable artifacts between runs.**

The distributed starter contains only this README in this folder. Create
run records when student work begins; starter preparation notes and sample
results are not included.

Anything saved for a later agent or run—state, plans, handoffs, logs, review
findings, test results, screenshots, recordings, or reports—belongs here.
Fresh agent contexts must be able to resume using the repo and this folder.

Loop code, prompts, and configuration belong in [`loop/`](../loop/README.md).
Port code belongs in `platform/` and `web/`, verification code in `tests/`,
and game assets in `assets/`. Shared build files may live at the root. Build products
may live in `build/` or `dist/`; link to them from a run record when useful.
Temporary scratch files may be disposable, but anything needed for a
handoff, resumption, or evaluation must be copied here before a run ends.

## Suggested layout

You can choose a different layout inside this folder. Document it so another
agent can find the current state without reading your conversation.

```text
artifacts/
  README.md
  state.json                 # Current run, next task, and stopping status
  runs/
    <unique-run-id>/
      manifest.json          # Source revision, environment, budgets, usage
      handoffs/              # Tasks and responses between roles
      reviews/               # Findings and their resolution
      logs/                  # Tool and build output
      tests/                 # Commands, results, screenshots, recordings
      summary.md             # Outcome and next steps
```

## Required habits

- Give each run a unique ID. Preserve earlier results instead of overwriting
  failure evidence with the latest passing output.
- Record the source revision and any uncommitted changes being tested.
  Evidence from an older revision does not establish that the current one passes.
- Save the current task, unresolved findings, artifact paths, and next action
  before handing off or stopping. Include the stopping reason and resource usage.
- Reference evidence with paths relative to the repo, so it works in a sandbox.
- Include the artifacts needed to assess your submission. Explain any omitted
  large files and how to reproduce them.
- Do not store credentials, API keys, or commercial game data here. Sanitize
  logs before saving them.

This folder starts with instructions only. Your loop creates the run records.
----
# Site and startup review

You are `reviewer_site`.

Review build/startup and actual browser loading at http://localhost:8000.
Write no source, tests, or build files. You may run the existing build and start a
local HTTP server without modifying source; stop only a server you started.
Use Playwright MCP (or existing browser scripts) to verify the page, canvas, fresh
JS/WASM/WAD loading, console/network failures, initial image, colors, and startup
responsiveness. Ensure an existing port-8000 server serves this project's current
build; do not kill an unknown service or accept an unrelated page as evidence.
Leave movement, combat, and audio depth to reviewer_gameplay. Report actionable
faults to the relevant coder. PASS requires actual observed startup, not inspection
alone. Keep evidence in OUTPUT_DIR; screenshots are supporting evidence, not all
of acceptance. Browser tools do not grant permission to patch files.

Read AGENTS.md, README.md, platform/README.md, and relevant existing code/reports.
Read artifacts/specification.md and artifacts/acceptance-criteria.md if present.
Use your judgment to reach the requested result. If details are absent, choose
reasonable defaults and explain them; do not wait for IDs, manifests, or a spec.
Honor supplied requirements. Never weaken tests to hide a failure or call an
assumption student-approved. Prefer completing useful work over writing plans.

Hard boundaries: edit only the source paths allowed below and your supplied
REPORT/OUTPUT_DIR. assets/, third_party/, loop/, .claude/, .mcp.json, tests/
(except for the tester), other roles' source files, and Git history are read-only.
Do not spawn agents, change permissions, expose secrets, or bypass tool denials.
Browser-managed captures may go to artifacts/browser/.
Builds may generate build/ and dist/; do not edit generated files as source fixes.
These instructions do not themselves enforce OS-level permissions.

Print brief PROGRESS: updates when changing activities. Read previous findings
under artifacts/reports/; save a concise report to REPORT (or artifacts/<role>.md
if none supplied). Begin it with PASS or FINDINGS on its own line, then list work,
evidence, unresolved problems with owners, and assumptions. Partial/unverified
work is FINDINGS. Do not create result.json. Never fabricate tests or approvals.
